{
  "abstract": "A Lean 4 / Mathlib formalization of the domination inequality (Lemma dom of P. Keevash, \"The existence of designs II\", arXiv:1802.05900, recalled there from \"The existence of designs\", Lemma 2.4; a form of Freedman's inequality) for random variables on a finite product of independent uniformly random permutations. All statements are exact finite inequalities on the space of all tuples (ω_c)_{c∈ι} of permutations ω_c of [a_c], ι finite, with the uniform measure. (1) DesignsII.freedman_perm: let F be a real function of the tuple, b_c ≥ 0 with b_c ≤ C (C > 0) such that changing the coordinate c changes F by at most b_c, and m_c such that for each c either b_c ≤ m_c, or F depends on ω_c only through an event E_c ⊆ Perm([a_c]) and 2·P(E_c)·b_c ≤ m_c; if ∑_c m_c > 0, then for every λ > 0, P(F − E F ≥ λ) ≤ exp(−λ²/(2C(λ + ∑_c m_c))). (2) DesignsII.count_tail_perm: for a counting variable X = #{β ∈ D : Ev_β}, if each event Ev_β (β ∈ D) is unaffected by the coordinates c with β ∉ Inf_c, |D ∩ Inf_c| ≤ C for every c, and for each c either |D ∩ Inf_c| ≤ m_c or the events Ev_β (β ∈ D ∩ Inf_c) depend on ω_c only through an event E_c with 2·P(E_c)·|D ∩ Inf_c| ≤ m_c, then, if ∑_c m_c > 0, for every λ > 0, P(|X − E X| ≥ λ) ≤ 2·exp(−λ²/(2C(λ + ∑_c m_c))). The point of these variance-sensitive bounds, as opposed to the bounded-differences inequality (Lemma lip3 of the paper, which gives P(|X − E X| > t) ≤ 2exp(−t²/(2B)) with B = ∑_c a_c b_c²), is that a coordinate whose change can move X by a large amount b_c, but only with small probability, contributes 2·P(E_c)·b_c instead of a_c·b_c² to the variance proxy; in the nibble of the paper (Lemma 3.9) the degree of an atom is a count of copies whose activation coordinates have exactly this form, and the bounded-differences bound is too weak there when q − r ≥ 2. Mathlib does not contain these results.",
